OCGRAPH 1
DOMAIN gameplay
# A THING THAT REACTS TO BEING HIT, and remembers how often. Still no C# anywhere in this project.
#
# Two capabilities meet here. ENTRY OnHit has existed since 75be56b, but until 6d7ff99 nothing could
# SEND one from a graph -- GraphHost.Fire was reachable only from C#, so a graph could declare a
# reaction it had no way of ever receiving. And VAR (a45e32f) is what lets the count survive the
# tick, stored per GraphHost instance, so two targets placed from this one file keep separate scores
# rather than sharing one.
NAME Target
DESCRIPTION Counts the hits it receives. One file, any number of instances, independent counts.

CLASS AN_Target Character

PARAM entity int

# FLOAT, not int, and the engine told me why: Add takes float pins and there is no integer
# Add in the palette, so an int VAR wired into one fails at load with
#   "Link prev.value (Int) to next.a (Float): type mismatch"
# -- a good error, caught before anything ran rather than counting wrongly at runtime.
VAR hits float 0

# ---- becoming raycast-visible ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHANGED FROM `Actor` TO `Character`, and that is not cosmetic: a plain Actor gets no physics body
# from ANYWHERE in this engine when it is spawned as a class instance -- LevelInstance.cpp's own
# static-box path (the one that stamps a level placement's body with its entity, per the
# raycast-entity slice) is deliberately skipped for a `class=` placement like this one, and nothing in
# ClassBuilder (Aver.Framework/ClassBuilder.cs) can add a collider either. `Character` is the one
# class in this engine whose graph-facing node reaches all the way down to a real Jolt body --
# AverCharacter.EnsureCapsule (Character.cs), reached through CharacterMove -- and, since this same
# slice, that capsule is stamped with its OWNING ENTITY the instant it exists
# (`Phys.aver_phys_set_entity(_capsule, Self.Handle)`, Character.cs:260) with no extra graph work
# needed here. `CharacterVirtual` is otherwise invisible to a raycast entirely -- Jolt does not add it
# to the broad phase on its own -- which the physics half of this slice fixed at the ABI level
# (aver_phys_character_create now gives every character a real, broadphase-visible inner body).
#
# ONE CALL, AT OnStart, WITH EVERY MOTION INPUT AT 0. EnsureCapsule is lazy -- first call creates it --
# so CharacterMove has to run at least once for this target to exist physically at all; it never needs
# to run again; wiring it into OnTick instead would just re-walk the same zero-motion command every
# frame for no benefit. This target does not move itself and does not look anywhere -- it only ever
# reacts to being hit.
#
# TWO HONEST CONSEQUENCES OF REUSING CharacterMove FOR THIS, NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. First: nothing
# in this map gives ANY character a floor to stand on -- AN_Player's own capsule already falls under
# gravity the same way once it exists -- so this target does too, the moment its own capsule is made;
# that is a property of this test range having no ground, not something this node introduces. Second:
# AverCharacter.Drive (which CharacterMove reaches through) unconditionally calls
# `Fw.aver_fw_set_view_entity` as part of creating its view node -- a single GLOBAL "the camera follows
# this entity" request (`FrameworkAbi.cpp`'s `viewRequest().entity`), not scoped to whichever character
# is actually possessed. Firing this ONCE, at load, means the camera can flash onto this target for at
# most the instant between its own OnStart and the player's own next CharacterMove tick reasserting
# itself -- CharacterMove has no "just make the capsule, leave the camera alone" mode to call instead,
# because no such node exists in this catalog. A future node that separates the two would remove this
# caveat; nothing here pretends the caveat is not real in the meantime.
ENTRY seed OnStart
NODE seed OnStart
NODE ent Param param=entity
NODE zero ConstFloat value=0.0
NODE settle CharacterMove
LINK seed.exec settle.exec
LINK ent.value settle.entity
LINK zero.value settle.dt
LINK zero.value settle.forward
LINK zero.value settle.right
LINK zero.value settle.yawDelta
LINK zero.value settle.pitchDelta

# ---- the reaction --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An ENTRY is not a node type: it names which node begins a chain, and OnHit differs from OnStart and
# OnTick only in that nobody calls it for you. A host -- or, since 6d7ff99, another graph's FireEvent
# -- has to fire it by name.
ENTRY hit OnHit
NODE hit OnHit

NODE one ConstFloat
PIN one value out float 1.0

NODE prev GetVar var=hits
NODE next Add
LINK prev.value next.a
LINK one.value next.b

NODE store SetVar var=hits
LINK hit.exec store.exec
LINK next.result store.value

# ---- reporting -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A SEPARATE GetVar, deliberately not the `prev` node above. An OUT record pulls a pure data
# expression, and pulling `prev` would report the count as it was BEFORE this hit was stored -- an
# off-by-one that looks like a lost hit. Reading through its own node after the write is the honest
# shape, and the same trap caught a reviewer building acceptance evidence for this very feature.
ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick
NODE report GetVar var=hits

OUT report value
